; Minimal probe for stringSplit (contract entry 39): one node, several receivers.
;
; Empty tokens. Contract §2.39 (A4) adjudicates `split` to **skip** empty tokens,
; so "a,,b" has two parts rather than three. The interpreter used to keep them --
; `StdlibPini.split` appended its accumulator unconditionally -- and that
; interpreter-side alignment landed with this fixture's own grid. Until then the
; two channels disagreed on every input below, which is why the empty-token case
; could not live in the corpus; the note that used to stand here said as much,
; and this paragraph is what replaces it.
;
; Why `len` is printed next to every array: a zero-element array and a
; one-element array holding the empty string print identically, so a fixture that
; printed only the array could not tell those two apart at all. The empty
; receiver is here for that same reason -- it is the one input where the element
; count, not the text, carries the verdict.
;
; What is covered: a multi-part split, a single-part split (no delimiter
; present), the element count, a delimiter that appears exactly once, and the
; empty-token family (doubled, leading, trailing, separators only, empty
; receiver). The array shape is as much a part of this node as the text is.
;
; What it still does *not* cover, stated so the gap reads as a gap rather than as
; coverage: the LLVM arm carries no guard for an empty separator, and it reads
; the separator as a character *set* rather than a substring. So "abc" split by
; "" and "a:b" split by "::" are two places where it parts company with the rule
; adjudicated above, and neither input can be added here -- a fixture holding
; either would fail, not because this node is wrong, but because the
; disagreement is real and filed against its own grid. Consequence worth stating
; plainly: the corpus still cannot tell a separator-substring split from a
; separator-character-set split, and that blind spot is a known one rather than
; an omission.

main|func() -> ():
    var s = "Hello, World"
    var parts = s.split(",")
    print(parts)
    print(len(parts))
    var more = "a-b-c".split("-")
    print(more)
    print(len(more))
    var none = "abc".split("-")
    print(none)
    print(len(none))
    var once = "a-b".split("-")
    print(once)
    print(len(once))
    var doubled = "a,,b".split(",")
    print(doubled)
    print(len(doubled))
    var leading = ",a".split(",")
    print(leading)
    print(len(leading))
    var trailing = "a,".split(",")
    print(trailing)
    print(len(trailing))
    var onlysep = ",,".split(",")
    print(onlysep)
    print(len(onlysep))
    var blank = "".split(",")
    print(blank)
    print(len(blank))
    return
